WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.640
ZJ: Hi, I am ZJ.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.730
I'm a data scientist
based in Melbourne.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:13.310
I want to talk about this
package called disk.frame.

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:15.950
Have you ever
encountered this message?

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:19.040
"Cannot allocate vectors
of size" whatever.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.960
So what is the issue here?

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:22.670
Well the issue is
that R trying to load

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:29.030
the whole data into RAM, so it
doesn't really fit sometimes.

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:31.580
If your data is quite large,
how do you deal with that?

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.140
In this talk, I talk
about disk.frame

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:36.530
but Apache Spark, via,
for example, sparklyr,

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:39.770
is also possible.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:42.010
So how does disk.frame work?

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:44.090
Well, disk.frame is very simple.

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:47.240
It's just a folder
with many fst files.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:51.670
If you're not familiar with
fst files, see this website.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:54.070
Each file is called a chunk.

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:57.580
And basically, if your
data set is too large,

00:00:57.580 --> 00:00:59.770
I don't have to load
everything through memory,

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:03.550
I just break that whole
dataset into smaller chunks

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:06.340
and load it chunk by chunk.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.860
Now, once I can load
the data chunk by chunk,

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:10.160
I can do many things.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.020
For example, I can operate
on the chunks in parallel.

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:20.190
So how do you make
use of disk.frame?

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:21.690
Well, the first
thing you want to do

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:24.730
is load some data into
the disk.frame format.

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:27.750
So the disk.frame package has
this convenient function called

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.460
csv_to_disk.frame.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:32.460
You simply provide a
path to the CSV file

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.820
and it'll convert
that to a disk.frame.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:41.460
If you have multiple CSVs, you
can just pass it a list of CSVs

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.575
or a vector of CSV paths.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.110
Well, I want to show you a
demo using 1.8 billion rows.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:51.215
So I've downloaded the
Fannie Mae dataset.

00:01:51.215 --> 00:01:52.590
So the first thing
you should do,

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:54.673
when you start up disk.frame,
is run this function

00:01:54.673 --> 00:01:55.890
called setup_disk.frame.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:58.213
This will set up parallel
processing for you.

00:01:58.213 --> 00:02:00.630
As I mentioned before, I've
created a folder with many fst

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:03.820
files in this directory.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:07.090
So I can show you the
content of this directory.

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:11.730
As you can see, it's just
a bunch of fst files.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.610
So to make use of
these fst files,

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:17.400
I just call this function
called disk.frame,

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.580
and I'm going to cut
the head and tail, just

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:21.840
like a normal data frame.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.470
I can see things
like the end row.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:27.120
So you can see, there's 1.8
billion rows in this dataset

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.535
and the CSV unzipped would
be about 200 gig in size.

00:02:32.535 --> 00:02:35.940
So these are more functions,
number of columns,

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:37.540
and then the names
of the columns.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:40.590
So you can operate a
disk.frame almost like

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.120
as if you have a data frame.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.980
One of the key
features of disk.frame

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:48.130
is that I've implemented
the dplyr verbs.

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:49.830
So you can make
use of dplyr verbs

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.530
directly to manipulate the data.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:55.590
So I start with the
disk.frame I created.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:57.600
I run this function
called srckeep,

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:58.840
and this is very important.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.470
This is actually what
makes disk.frame fast.

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:05.100
So a srckeep basically
tells the program

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.410
which columns to
load into memory,

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:08.970
because there's 31 columns.

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:13.050
For this particular analysis,
I only need these four columns.

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:17.250
So I only need to load
these four columns into RAM.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:22.740
So I just filter like you
would normally do with dplyr,

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:26.730
do a mutate and then group_by
year, which is created here,

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:29.070
and do a summarize,
and finally collect.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.920
So let's run that.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:35.490
While that's running,
I will show you

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:39.560
what's happening with
the task manager.

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:41.810
Most of the CPUs
are being utilized,

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:43.910
and that's because it's
processing all the chunks

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:45.245
in that dataset in parallel.

00:03:45.245 --> 00:03:48.350
Well, as you can see,
I've done a group_by

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:52.220
and created new columns in the
meantime in about one minute.

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:55.850
This was done on the Fannie Mae
dataset with 1.8 billion rows.

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:59.090
To learn more about disk.frame,
simply go to diskframe.com.

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:02.000
I'd like to finish
off by pointing out

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:03.990
a couple of things.

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:08.490
If you do have Spark, it's
perfectly fine to use it,

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:12.770
but not everyone's lucky enough
to have a Spark cluster set up.

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:15.710
So disk.frame, you can
use R functions directly,

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:17.700
even those defined by the user.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.449
Thank you.

